  "query_preview": "into the early afternoon. The anomalously warm airmass \nin place, 850 mb temps of 8-10 C, alongside positive height anomalies \ncontinue the well above normal temps. The lingering stratus in the \neast will complicate temp forecasts this afternoon, limiting warming \ninto the mid 50s to low 60s (~10 F above normal). The west will be \nwarmer in the mid 60s to mid 70s (15-25 F above normal). Otherwise \ndry weather with light southerly winds 5-10 mph with gusts mainly \n~15 mph. Unlike the past nights, winds stay light out of the south \nwith gusts in downslope areas around 15-20 mph. Temps settle into \nthe 40s to mid 50s, warmest west where there is a brief low chance \nfor light rain showers (15-20% chance).\n\nBreezy and warm on Saturday. A low pressure tracking over northern \nManitoba and Hudson Bay this weekend tightens the pressure gradient \nand increases winds to 10-20 mph with gusts of 25-35 mph. Strongest \nwinds are expected in areas of south downslope flow, particularly in \nthe eastern Lake Superior shoreline areas. 850 mb temps climb \nfurther to 11-13 C, continuing well above normal temps (+10-25 F) \nwith highs in the 60s to mid 70s, warmest west. This combination \nheightens fire weather concerns, thankfully mostly cloudy skies \nlimit Td mixing and RHs remain above 30%, limiting elevated fire \nweather conditions.\n\nA cold front sweeps across the UP from west to east Saturday night \ninto Sunday, bringing a chance (20-40%) for rain showers. \nAccumulations around or below 0.1 inches are expected; probabilities \nexceeding this amount are only 5-25%. Winds turn west behind the \nfront with cooler near normal temps peaking in the upper 40s to low \n60s, coldest northwest and warmest south-central (generally 5-10 F \nabove normal). As drier air presses in, clouds clear and mixing \nlowers RHs in the west into the 20-30% range. Higher minimum RH in \nthe upper 30s to mid 60s is expected in the Keweenaw and east. Winds \nlook to remain under 15 mph at this time where RH is lowest (only a \n10-30% chance for wind gusts exceeding 20 mph coupled with RH below \n30%), limiting fire weather concerns. Will continue to monitor this \nperiod.\n\nHigh pressure passes east over Ontario and the Great lakes Monday \nand Tuesday. Then flow aloft turns zonal, allowing for multiple \nshortwave troughs to track east next week into the following weekend. \nGuidance begins to struggle to resolve this pattern already by \nTuesday night, but this likely sends a low pressure system just to \nthe northwest of the Great Lakes mid to late next week. The \nshortwaves, low pressure system, and late week colder airmass \nyielding sufficient delta-Ts for lake effect precip keeps PoPs in \nmost of the extended forecast. Otherwise, temps return near normal \nin the wake of the cold front on Sunday peaking in the 40s to low \n50s Monday and Tuesday. Temps Monday night settle into the 20s to \nmid 30s, coldest interior west. Southerly flow returning for \nWednesday and Thursday warm temps into the 50s with lows in the 30s \nand 40s.",
